## DESCRIPTION
## Linear Algebra
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by cmd6a 4/29/06

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Euclidean spaces)
## DBsection(Coordinate vectors and change of basis)
## Date(July 2013)
## Institution(Rochester and Hope College)
## Author(Paul Pearson)
## Level(3)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Linear Algebra with Applications')
## AuthorText1('Jeffrey Holt')
## EditionText1('1')
## Section1('6.3')
## Problem1('')

DOCUMENT() ;        

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserVectorUtils.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1 ;

TEXT(beginproblem());

Context('Matrix');

$b11 = random(3,5,1);
$b12 = non_zero_random(-2,2,1);
$b21 = random(-3,3,1);
$b22 = random(3,5,2);

## matrix b is invertible

$r11 = random(-2,2,4);
$r12 = random(-3,3,2);
$r21 = 3/$r12;
$r22 = $r11;

## determinant of matrix r is 1.

$i11 = $r11;
$i12 = - $r12;
$i21 = - $r21; 
$i22 = $r11;

$p11 = $i11*$b11 + $i12*$b21;
$p12 = $i11*$b12 + $i12*$b22;
$p21 = $i21*$b11 + $i22*$b21;
$p22 = $i21*$b12 + $i22*$b22;

$B1 = Matrix([[$b11], [$b21]]);
$B2 = Matrix([[$b12], [$b22]]);

$C1 = Matrix([[1], [0]]);
$C2 = Matrix([[0], [1]]);

$P = Matrix([[$b11,$b12],[$b21,$b22]]);
$d = $b11*$b22-$b12*$b21;
$Q= Matrix([[$b22/$d,-$b12/$d],[-$b21/$d,$b11/$d]]);


Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
This problem ask you to find the change-of-basis matrix and its inverse 
(although I'm not saying which is first and which is second.)
Try finding the inverse by hand for practice.  You are welcome to use SAGE to check.
$BR
$HR
$BR

Let \(B = \left\lbrace $B1, $B2 \right\rbrace \).
Find the matrix \(P\) such that \( \vec{x} = P [\vec{x}]_B \) for all \(\vec{x}\) in \( {\mathbb R}^2 \). 
$BCENTER
\(P=\) \{ $P->ans_array \}
$ECENTER
$BR
$HR
$BR
Find the matrix \(Q\) such that  \( [\vec{x}]_B = Q\vec{x}  \) for all \(\vec{x}\) in \( {\mathbb R}^2 \). 
$BCENTER
\(Q=\) \{ $Q->ans_array \}
$ECENTER


END_TEXT
Context()->normalStrings;

ANS($P->cmp);
ANS($Q->cmp);
;
ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
